\maestro sets up conducting LiveLily with a sensor that
sends its data via OSC (for example an accelerometer held
by a conductor). The data is received on port 9050.
It does nothing by itself and takes no arguments, you must
call one of its methods, concatenated with a dot.

\maestro.mainaddr sets the OSC address where the sensor
data is received (default /maestro):

\maestro.mainaddr /acc

\maestro.valndx sets which value of the OSC message is
read (default 0), and \maestro.valthresh sets the threshold
above which a value triggers a beat (default 7.5):

\maestro.valndx 2
\maestro.valthresh 9.5

Each beat moves the score on by one beat, and the tempo of
the bar is set from the time between two beats. The first
beat only starts the counting.

\maestro.toggleaddr sets an OSC address that receives a 1
or a 0 to start or stop receiving beats (default /toggle).
If it is set, receiving starts only with a 1 on this
address, otherwise it starts with \maestro.mainaddr.

\maestro.toggleaddr /conduct

\maestro.levareaddr sets an OSC address that receives an
integer, the number of beats to count in when receiving is
turned on (default /levare, see \score.beatsin).

Several methods can be written in one line, like:

\maestro.mainaddr /acc valndx 2 valthresh 9.5

Each method takes one argument, a missing one results in
an error.
